P1-7 — the queue's RPO, measured
================================

Run:    2026-08-17
Drill:  bench/drill-p17.sh
Server: TimeLakeDB deploy/compose/timelakedb.yml (plaintext, single node)
Agent:  tributary release build, run from a debian:trixie-slim container on
        the compose network

ROADMAP §5 open question 2 says to MEASURE queue durability on ephemeral
nodes before recommending anything. This is that measurement.

Two failure models, deliberately not conflated
----------------------------------------------

RESTART — the process dies, the node and its disk come back.

    written            : 8000
    acked after restart: 8000
    RPO                : 0 lines — the checkpoint resumed exactly

  This is L1's property, re-checked because P1-7's whole claim is that the
  two cases are different. On a durable disk the queue and checkpoint are
  still there, so nothing is lost.

NODE LOSS — the node vanishes: spot eviction, a terminated container with an
emptyDir. Everything unacked goes with it, including the source log files,
which is the term people forget when they reason about the queue alone.

  profile                               rate/s   sample     peak    BOUND
  default     (batch 2000, inflight 4)    2000      200     1000    10000
  small-batch (batch 100,  inflight 1)    2000      400      100      200
  default     (batch 2000, inflight 4)     200       20       80    10000
  small-batch (batch 100,  inflight 1)     200      100      100      200

  sample = lines lost by ONE `kill -9`.
  peak   = the highest at-risk total the agent itself reported during the
           run, read back out of its own "at risk if this node is lost now"
           lines.
  BOUND  = batch_lines * (1 + max_inflight).

Read the BOUND column, not the sample column
--------------------------------------------

The first version of this drill reported only the sample, and it said the
small-batch profile was WORSE — 400 lines lost against the default's 200.
That is a real number and a useless one: a single kill lands at an arbitrary
point on the flush sawtooth, so it measures when the kill happened at least
as much as how the agent was configured. Two profiles were compared on one
draw each from different distributions.

The hypothesis that followed — "small batches lose more because throughput
falls below the arrival rate" — was then tested by re-running both profiles
at a tenth of the rate, where the small-batch profile comfortably keeps up.
It lost more there too (100 against 20). **The hypothesis was wrong**, and
the samples were noise.

Instrumenting the agent's own exposure settled it. By peak observed
exposure the small-batch profile is an order of magnitude better at the full
rate (100 lines against 1000), and by config bound it is fifty times better
(200 against 10,000). The sample column is kept in the drill output, labelled
as an anecdote, because deleting the misleading number would hide why the
bound is the thing worth quoting.

The RPO, stated
---------------

    Durable disk, process restart      RPO = 0            (L1, re-verified)
    Node loss                          RPO <= batch_lines * (1 + max_inflight)
                                             + whatever the queue is holding

The queue term is zero in steady state — it fills only while the server is
refusing writes, which is what it is for. `queue_max_bytes` bounds it.

Worked examples at the shipped defaults (batch_lines 5000, max_inflight 4):

    bound = 5000 * 5 = 25,000 lines
    at  1,000 lines/s  ->  25 s of data
    at 10,000 lines/s  ->   2.5 s of data

Both figures are the ceiling, not the expectation: observed peaks ran ~10x
under the bound in this drill.

What an operator should do with this
------------------------------------

* On a durable disk, the queue does what it says and the RPO is zero for a
  process restart.
* On an ephemeral node, choose the bound explicitly. `batch_lines` and
  `max_inflight` are the two knobs; halving either halves the ceiling.
* Watch the agent's own line — it prints the live exposure every
  `rpo_report_secs` (default 60):

    INFO tributary: at risk if this node is lost now pending_lines=800
    inflight_batches=0 queue_segments=0 queue_bytes=0 unread_bytes=0

  A non-zero `queue_bytes` means the server has been refusing writes and the
  exposure now includes the spool. A growing `unread_bytes` means the source
  is outrunning the agent, and on an ephemeral node those unread bytes are
  as lost as the queued ones.

The cost of the small-batch profile is throughput, and L3 measured that
separately (156k -> 492k lines/s once the checkpoint stopped being the
bottleneck). This drill deliberately does not restate it: one number per
repository, measured where it belongs.
